    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented optional, city-region-validated `postcode` for owners, satisfying the specification and all three acceptance tests (`coreStoresValidPostcode`, `coreAcceptsOwnerWithoutPostcode`, `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode`). Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS, 3/3 passing**.\n\n### Changes\n\n**Model** \u2014 `model/Owner.java`\n- Added a persisted `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`) with `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{4}$\")` so a malformed postcode is rejected via Bean Validation (400), plus its getter/setter. Reused the existing `getRegion()` city\u2192region derivation.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `resources/openapi.yml`\n- Added `postcode` to the `OwnerFields` schema (`type: string`, `pattern: ^[0-9]{4}$`), not marked required so it stays backward-compatible. Because `Owner` is `allOf` `OwnerFields`, the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` both carry it, and MapStruct auto-maps it by name in both directions (store on create, return on read).\n\n**Region-range check** \u2014 `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`\n- Added `isPostcodeValidForCity(owner)` gated in `addOwner`: null postcode is accepted; when present it must be 4 digits and, when the city maps to a known region, must fall within `NSW 2000\u20132099 / VIC 3000\u20133099 / QLD 4000\u20134099`; an unknown region accepts any 4-digit postcode. Out-of-range \u2192 400. This mirrors the existing controller-side validation style (email/telephone).\n\n**Schema + seed data** \u2014 `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`, `db/{hsqldb,mysql}/data.sql`\n- Appended a `postcode` column at the end of the `owners` table in all four dialects (matching the codebase's convention of appending new columns like `customer_code`/`identity_key`). Updated the two positional-insert seed files (hsqldb \u2014 the one used by tests \u2014 and mysql) to add a trailing `NULL`; h2 and postgres use explicit column lists and needed no change.\n\nOne thing worth noting: the `mysql/data.sql` owner inserts were already positional with fewer values than the table has columns (pre-existing, and the mysql profile isn't exercised by the test suite). I kept it consistent by appending a `NULL` but did not otherwise reconcile that pre-existing mismatch, since it's outside this change's scope.",
